<h1>Introduction to INTERNET</h1> 
The international forum for project management<p>
<p>
<b><p>
The History</b><p>
<p>
<p>
INTERNET - International Project Management Association is a non-profit making
organization registered in Z&uuml;rich as an international Association under
Swiss law.<p>
<p>
<p>
Its objective is to improve, on an international basis, the ability and the
will of people and of organizations to work together in managing resources, and
to plan and implement important development projects and programmes.<p>
<p>
<p>
In 1965 the Association began in Europe as a discussion group of managers of
international projects who were concerned with the increasing difficulty of
managing large projects and scarce resources. The first International Congress
was held 1967 in Vienna and participants came from 30 different countries.
Since that time, INTERNET has developed steadily.<p>
<p>
<b><p>
<p>
The Goals and Activities</b><p>
<p>
The International Project Management Associations is continuing to<p>
<ul>
<li> develop the association on a worldwide basis, as a viable organization of
practitioners and academics concerned with the problem of managing projects,
operations and programmes.<p>
<li> provide an international forum for the exchange of ideas and experiences, and
to identify and explore problems and requirements<p>
<li> provide international guidelines and standards for education and practice in
managing developmental work<p>
<li> promote international friendship, trust, goodwill and cooperation between
professionals and educators in project management<p>
<li> promote the development and use of improved management tools<p>
<li> explore and resolve the cross-cultural communication problems of
international projects<p>
<li> improve the recognition and acceptance of project management concepts and
methods<p>
<li> improve the general management enviroment in whcih the projct manager is
working 
</ul>
<p><b>The Members<p>
<p>
</b><p>
A most significant INTERNET characteristic is the parallel development of
associated national societies which serve the specific development needs of
each country in its national language. INTENRET has thus emerged as an
itnernational association of national project management societies.<p>
<p>
<p>
Member societies have been established in<p>
<p>
<ul>
<li> Austria	o	Iceland<p>
<li> Czechoslovakia	o	Italy<p>
<li> Denmark	o	The Netherlands<p>
<li> Egypt	o	Norway<p>
<li> Finland	o	Russia<p>
<li> France	o	Spain<p>
<li> Germany	o	Sweden<p>
<li> Greece	o	United Kingdom<p>
<li> Hungary<p>
</ul>
<p>
New Societies are being founded in the new states of Eastern Europe (Slovenia,
Kroatia, the Baltics, Ukraine, etc.).<p>
<p>
INTERNET also has a continuing cooperation with the Project Management
Institute of North America (PMI), the Engineering Advancement Association of
Japan (ENAA), the International Associations of Cost Engineers and Project
Management Associations in Australia.<p>
<b><p>
<p>
<p>
The Organization</b><p>
<p>
The national societies, the exclusive members, the individual and corporate
members of INTERNET elect the representatives to the Council of Delegates which
has the function of a general assembly, meeting once a year to decide on
matters of importance to the association. It elects its Chairman and the
President and the Auditors of the Association.<p>
<p>
The INTERNET International Board is responsible for the management of the
association and it sets the guidelines for its activities. The Board
constitutes itself and appoints the Executive Committee and the manager of the
International Secretariat. The International Secretariat with its officers in
Z&uuml;rich/Switzerland is the centre of communication for the National
Organizations and for all INTERNET bodies and members.
<p><b>The Culture</b><p>
<p>
<p>
An important INTERNET function is the international exchange of ideas,
attitudes and experiences. Communication is being improved by presenting new
types of congress and seminar work and by producing new international
publications on project management.<p>
<p>
Special emphasis is given to the discussion
between the scientists of the academic world and the experts from industry.<p>
<p>
<b><p>
<p>
The Seminars</b><p>
<p>
<p>
Fourteen International Expert Seminars on selected management topics have been
held since 1971 at the Gottlieb Duttweiler Institute (GDI) in Rueschlikon near
Z&uuml;rich.<p>
<p>
1971	"Practical Application of Proejct Network Techniques"<p>
1973	"Project and Operations Management Systems, Methods and Techniques"<p>
1975	"Improving Project, Oeprations and Systems Management"<p>
1975	"Cost Control in Project Management"<p>
